<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Inflanty Voivodeship</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Polish Livonia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Inflanty Voivodeship was a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> formed from the part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> that Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> kept after its 17th-century wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, roughly corresponding to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgale">Latgale</a> in modern south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>. Its seat was Dyneburg (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daugavpils">Daugavpils</a>). It was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inflanty-voivodeship.jpg" alt="Inflanty Voivodeship" width="320" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mathiasrex, based on layers of Halibutt · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IRP_Livland_voivodship.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name Inflanty comes through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polonisation">Polonization</a> from Livland, the German name for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>. Zygmunt Gloger, in his Historical Geography of the Lands of Old Poland, describes the land as inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a>, whose language is close to Lithuanian, and says that it and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> were conquered in the 13th century by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a>. The voivodeship was one of the few territories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> ruled jointly by Poland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>: since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> had made Livonia a joint possession, royal bills for the province bore both Polish and Lithuanian stamps.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Pressed by Muscovy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and the Commonwealth, Master Gotthard Kettler turned to the Polish king, and a document signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> on 28 November 1561 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> a Polish–Lithuanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>. A long struggle with Muscovy and later Sweden followed. After the victories of King Stephen Báthory, Commonwealth rule was confirmed in 1582 and the province divided into three presidencies centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Dorpat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesis">Wenden</a> and Parnawa, which King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-iii-vasa">Sigismund III Vasa</a> renamed voivodeships in 1598.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> took most of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> in the Polish–Swedish War of 1621–1625, and the Inflanty Voivodeship was formed in the 1620s from what was left of the Wenden Voivodeship. The Treaty of Oliwa of 1660 left the Commonwealth about one fifth of Livonia, despite the efforts of Jan Zamoyski and Jan Karol Chodkiewicz to hold it.</p><p>The Warsaw Sejm of 1677 gave the province the right to name three senators: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a>, the Voivode and the Castellan of Inflanty. Local sejmiks met at Dyneburg, and starostas resided at Dyneburg, Lucyn, Rzezyca and Marienhaus. Of its six deputies to the Sejm only two came from Inflanty; the king named the other four in memory of the lost part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>. Its noble families included descendants of German knights, such as the Borch, Plater and Tyzenhaus, and Polish or Lithuanian settlers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Inflanty Voivodeship” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Truce of Altmark in 1629 left most of the duchy as Swedish Livonia, while the south-east, Latgale, remained Polish as the <b>Inflanty Voivodeship</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-livonia">Duchy of Livonia</a></li></ul></section>
